# The restic build this fleet runs, and the hashes that prove it.
#
# THIS FILE HAS A TWIN: foundation/restic/pin.go. Change both, or CI fails.
#
# The Go copy is what actually installs restic on a machine — sion-backup
# downloads and verifies its own, into the per-user data directory, needing no
# administrator. This file is the same facts in a form that can be read before
# there is a Go toolchain or a binary: by CI, by the Makefile, and by anybody
# checking what a machine is supposed to be running. TestPinMatchesDeployFile
# compares the two and fails on drift.
#
# restic is the program that actually reads every file on somebody's computer
# and holds the credentials to their off-site copy. It is downloaded from the
# internet during installation. Verifying it is therefore not box-ticking: an
# unverified binary here is a compromise of the machine and of the bucket, and
# it is the single weakest link in an otherwise careful chain.
#
# These hashes are a subset of the upstream SHA256SUMS published with the
# release, copied verbatim:
#
#   https://github.com/restic/restic/releases/download/v0.19.1/SHA256SUMS
#
# restic signs that file. If you are bumping the version, check the signature
# before trusting the hashes you copy from it:
#
#   gpg --verify SHA256SUMS.asc SHA256SUMS
#
# The signing key is published at https://restic.net/gpg-key-alex.asc
#
# # Why pin at all
#
# Two reasons beyond supply chain. A pinned version means "which restic wrote
# this snapshot", recorded on every run, is a fact rather than a guess. And a
# repository written by a newer restic can require a newer restic to read —
# the owner's restore card promises "any version 0.14 or newer will read this
# backup", and that promise is only keepable if the fleet's version is known.
#
# # Format
#
# Shell-sourceable, and simple enough to parse with a regex. Keep it that way:
# this file is read by several different things and none of them should need a
# YAML parser. The installers no longer read it at all — they let the binary
# install restic, which is the one implementation of the download-and-verify
# dance that used to exist three times.

RESTIC_VERSION=0.19.1

RESTIC_SHA256_linux_amd64=f415415624dcc452f2a02b8c33641791a8c6d6d3b65bbb3543fcf9a25151585c
RESTIC_SHA256_linux_arm64=a5f64aaab53d51e311fa3829124c5b703f2d14cf187d8640b6be3b2b49376465
RESTIC_SHA256_darwin_amd64=c38d579622cf602f665234c5a8c315030b6cf70656028fe6dc29a786b60e5f35
RESTIC_SHA256_darwin_arm64=7be0a144ccc377880f294204aa271d76e4b79554b42a751151d425ce6ebac143
RESTIC_SHA256_windows_amd64=da948ad707ed690426473aaba2046cd61f8f90f6f0e7dab6be0d5796531de67d
